{"sections":[],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArpeggiate\/id","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlarpeggiate\/id"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The optional-unique-id attribute group allows an element to optionally specify an ID that is unique to the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"entire document. This attribute group is not used for a required id attribute, or for an id attribute that"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"specifies an id reference."}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"id","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:SS","text":"String","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"level":2,"anchor":"discussion","type":"heading","text":"Discussion"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Maps to the MusicXML ","type":"text"},{"code":"id","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" attribute.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"metadata":{"title":"id","role":"symbol","externalID":"s:7IvorMXL13MXLArpeggiateV2idSSSgvp","roleHeading":"Instance Property","fragments":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"id","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:SS","text":"String","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"modules":[{"name":"IvorMXL"}],"symbolKind":"property"},"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"kind":"symbol","hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArpeggiate"]]},"references":{"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL":{"type":"topic","title":"IvorMXL","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A MusicXML parser, normalizer, validator, and formatter."}],"role":"collection","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLArpeggiate/id":{"title":"id","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlarpeggiate\/id","abstract":[{"text":"The optional-unique-id attribute group allows an element to optionally specify an ID that is unique to the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"entire document. This attribute group is not used for a required id attribute, or for an id attribute that","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"specifies an id reference.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"id","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:SS","text":"String","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArpeggiate\/id","kind":"symbol"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLArpeggiate":{"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArpeggiate","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLArpeggiate"}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlarpeggiate","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLArpeggiate"}],"title":"MXLArpeggiate","abstract":[{"text":"The arpeggiate type indicates that this note is part of an arpeggiated chord. The number attribute can be used","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"to distinguish between two simultaneous chords arpeggiated separately (different numbers) or together (same","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"number). The direction attribute is used if there is an arrow on the arpeggio sign. By default, arpeggios go","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"from the lowest to highest note. The length of the sign can be determined from the position attributes for the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"arpeggiate elements used with the top and bottom notes of the arpeggiated chord. If the unbroken attribute is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"set to yes, it indicates that the arpeggio continues onto another staff within the part. This serves as a hint","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"to applications and is not required for cross-staff arpeggios.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic"}}}